<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hemachandra</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1088–1173</span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Hemacandra</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hemachandra was a Jain monk, poet and polymath at the court of the Chaulukya kings <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jayasimha-siddharaja">Jayasimha Siddharaja</a> and Kumarapala of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a>. He wrote grammars of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prakrit">Prakrit</a>, lexicons, works on poetics, the Trishashti-shalaka-purusha-charitra on Jain sacred history and the Yogashastra. His influence helped make Kumarapala a patron of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jainism">Jainism</a>, and he is regarded as a founding figure of Gujarati learning.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hemachandra.jpg" alt="Hemachandra" width="320" height="473" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: hi:User:अनुनाद सिंह · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hemachandra.gif" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
